    "category_specific_evidence": [
      "Special values involved: NaN (np.nan).",
      "Where they enter: convolution edge treatment via boundary='fill' with fill_value=np.nan; also via presence of NaN values in the input array (e.g., x or ar set to np.nan / ar[5,5]=np.nan).",
      "Special-value handling required: when nan_treatment=='interpolate', the code condition to enable NaN interpolation must also account for the case where the boundary is filled with NaNs (ignore NaNs).",
      "Code evidence: nan_interpolate is computed as (nan_treatment == 'interpolate') and ((boundary == 'fill' and np.isnan(fill_value)) or np.isnan(array_internal.sum())).",
      "Observed bug symptoms: convolve vs convolve_fft are inconsistent with NaNs at edges/inputs; convolve returns output still including NaNs even when interpolate_nan/preserve_nan is specified, while convolve_fft does not.",
      "Regression expectations: results should be consistent between convolve and convolve_fft, asserted with assert_array_almost_equal(..., decimal=10).",
      "Additional checks in the regression test: when input has a single NaN at ar[5,5], smoothed[5,5] is NaN and np.sum(np.isnan(smoothed)) == 1; same for smoothed_fft, and the arrays should match within decimal=10.",
      "Expected behavior statement (issue 16497): convolve should not have a NaN buffer of kernel_size//2 if no NaN value is present in the data; and output should not differ depending on whether NaN values are present (match convolve_fft)."
    ],
    "reproduction_risks": [
      "The passages only mention NaN as the special value; they do not provide evidence for +/-inf, signed zero, or subnormal handling, so including those would not be grounded."
    ]
